before:
```python
class Label:
    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text

    def render(self):
        return self.text

class FramedLabel(Label):
    def render(self):
        return "[" + super().render() + "]"

plain = Label("Map")
framed = FramedLabel("Map")
print(plain.render())
print(framed.render())
```
The outputs are Map and [Map]. Label.render returns its stored string. FramedLabel.render returns a new framed string, leaving self.text unchanged.

after:
```python
class Label:
    def __init__(self, text):
        self.text = text

    def render(self):
        return self.text

class FramedLabel(Label):
    def render(self):
        return "[" + super().render() + "]"

plain = Label("Map")
framed = FramedLabel("Map")
print(plain.render())
print(framed.render())
```
It prints `Map`, then `[Map]`. The base method returns the saved text. The new method puts brackets around it. The saved `self.text` stays unchanged.
